\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:method:problem}

Let $f_\theta$ denote a language model and $\mathcal{D}_0$ a corpus of human-written seed text.
We define a \emph{recursive generation chain} of depth $K$ as the sequence $\mathcal{D}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_K$, where each $\mathcal{D}_k$ ($k \geq 1$) is produced by finetuning $f_\theta$ on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ and sampling from the resulting model.
Depth $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text; depth $d{=}k$ ($k \geq 1$) denotes text generated by a model trained on depth-$(k{-}1)$ data.

Given a text passage $x$, the task is to estimate its recursive generation depth $d \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$.
This is an ordinal classification problem: depths carry a natural ordering, and $d{=}1$ is structurally closer to $d{=}0$ than $d{=}3$ is, since each generation step compounds distributional drift.

\subsection{Multi-Generational Benchmark Construction}
\label{sec:method:benchmark}

We construct a benchmark spanning three axes: model family, decoding strategy, and generation depth.

\paragraph{Model families.}
We select three 1--1.5B-parameter models with diverse pretraining corpora and architectures: Pythia-1.4B (EleutherAI), OLMo-1B (AI2), and GPT-2~XL (OpenAI, 1.5B).
Restricting to a narrow parameter range isolates the effect of architecture and training data from scale.

\paragraph{Decoding strategies.}
Each model is paired with three decoding strategies: nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$) \cite{holtzman2020_nucleus}, temperature sampling ($\tau{=}0.9$), and top-$k$ sampling ($k{=}50$).
These span the dominant modes of open-ended generation and induce qualitatively different entropy profiles.
Our findings on feature importance and discriminability are limited to this grid; transfer to untested models, scales, or strategies is not established.

\paragraph{Depth and scale.}
The primary benchmark covers depths d0--d3 across all $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells, with 5{,}000 samples per depth per cell (180K samples total).
For depth extension experiments (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), we extend one pipeline (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus) to d0--d5.

\paragraph{Generation pipeline.}
Starting from a Wikipedia subset as seed text ($\mathcal{D}_0$), each generation cycle proceeds in three steps: (1)~LoRA finetuning \cite{hu2022lora} on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$, freezing base model weights and training low-rank update matrices ($<$1\% additional parameters; $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, lr $2{\times}10^{-4}$, batch size 8, 3 epochs, dropout $0.05$, architecture-specific targets); (2)~sampling 5{,}000 passages from the LoRA-finetuned model (max 512 tokens, BOS-seeded, no prompt conditioning) under the specified decoding strategy; (3)~the generated corpus becomes $\mathcal{D}_k$ for the next cycle.
Each depth is produced by a model explicitly trained on the prior depth's data---not by sampling from the unmodified base model.
Our BOS-seeded generation may amplify depth signal relative to prompted settings; extending to prompt-conditioned generation is future work.
There is no per-passage lineage across depths, ensuring cross-validation splits do not introduce information leakage.

\paragraph{Reference model.}
All statistical features (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) are computed using a fixed reference model---Pythia-1.4B---providing a consistent perplexity reference across all generation conditions.
We select Pythia-1.4B because it is the smallest of the three generators, and its training corpus (The Pile) partially overlaps with those of OLMo and GPT-2~XL, keeping perplexity values in a comparable range.
For the Pythia pipeline, feature extraction constitutes self-model measurement; for OLMo and GPT-2~XL, it is cross-model.
Empirically, the Pythia pipeline's 4-class accuracy (78.2\%) does not exceed GPT-2~XL (85.4\%), indicating that self-model measurement does not confer a systematic advantage.
In cross-model leave-one-out evaluation (\S\ref{sec:generalization}), the reference model remains fixed regardless of which generator is held out; the implications of sharing the reference model with one generator are discussed in Limitations.
An ablation replacing Pythia-1.4B with OLMo-1B as reference model yields 74.5\% vs 78.5\%, suggesting that depth signal detection is not critically dependent on the specific reference model ($\Delta$=4.0pp, within the 5pp robustness threshold; Appendix~\ref{tab:ref_ablation}).

\subsection{Statistical Feature Design}
\label{sec:method:features}

We extract a feature vector $\mathbf{f}(x)$ comprising 19 candidate features from the reference-model perplexity distribution over each passage $x$; ablation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:features}) shows the top 5 achieve 97.6\% of full-set performance, and variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 features, placing the effective independent dimensionality at ${\approx}$4--5.
The features fall into four categories (Table~\ref{tab:features}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 19 statistical features comprising $\mathbf{f}(x)$, grouped by category. All perplexity and surprisal features are computed from the reference model (Pythia-1.4B).}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Category & Features & $n$ \\
\midrule
Perplexity & mean, var, skewness, kurtosis, & 9 \\
statistics & p10, p25, p50, p75, p90 & \\
\addlinespace
Surprisal & mean, var, entropy & 3 \\
statistics & & \\
\addlinespace
Lexical & type--token ratio, hapax ratio, & 4 \\
diversity & bigram entropy, trigram entropy & \\
\addlinespace
Repetition & repeated 2/3/4-gram fractions & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Perplexity statistics} (9 features) capture the per-token perplexity distribution via moments (mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis) and quantiles (p10--p90); tail quantiles prove especially informative as recursive finetuning erodes the high-perplexity tail (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).
\emph{Surprisal statistics} (3 features): mean, variance, and entropy of $-\log p(\text{token})$; surprisal variance captures second-order structure not redundant with perplexity moments.
\emph{Lexical diversity} (4 features): type--token ratio, hapax ratio, bigram/trigram entropy.
\emph{Repetition} (3 features): repeated $n$-gram fractions for $n \in \{2, 3, 4\}$.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:method:edd}

Aggregate multi-class accuracy obscures whether discriminability degrades at specific depth boundaries.
We introduce the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD) to formalize the frontier beyond which adjacent depths become statistically indistinguishable.

\paragraph{Pairwise discriminability.}
Let $\mathcal{D} = \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$ denote the depth set and $T \in (0.5, 1.0]$ a discrimination threshold.
For each adjacent pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we define the pairwise discriminability $\mathrm{AUC}(d, d{+}1)$ as the area under the ROC curve of a dedicated binary Random Forest classifier (500 trees, $\lfloor\sqrt{19}\rfloor$ candidate features per split) trained on class-balanced data from $\mathbf{f}(x)$ (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) to distinguish depth-$d$ from depth-$(d{+}1)$ samples, evaluated via 5-fold stratified cross-validation.
Each pair uses an independent classifier, avoiding the decision boundary dilution that arises in multi-class settings (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

\begin{definition}[Effective Discriminable Depth]
\label{def:edd}
The \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} at threshold $T$ is:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 \,:\, \forall\, d' \leq d^*,\; \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\label{eq:edd}
\end{equation}
where $d^* \in \{0, \dots, K{-}1\}$. If\, $\mathrm{AUC}(0,1) < T$, then $\mathrm{EDD}_T = 0$.
\end{definition}

$\mathrm{EDD}_T$ is the deepest depth $d$ such that every adjacent pair from $(0,1)$ through $(d{-}1, d)$ remains discriminable at level $T$.
The universal quantifier ensures EDD reports the longest \emph{contiguous} discriminable prefix rather than an isolated high-AUC pair at greater depth.\footnote{Our AUC profiles are empirically monotonically decreasing for the 1--1.5B base models (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), so in practice $\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max\{d'{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T\}$. Qwen2.5-7B exhibits a U-shaped AUC pattern rather than monotonic decay, possibly reflecting instruction-tuning effects (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}). For non-monotonic profiles, the contiguous formulation is more conservative (see Limitations).}
We set $T{=}0.75$ as default; sensitivity at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ is reported in \S\ref{sec:atlas}.

\paragraph{Classifiers.}
Our primary classifier is a Random Forest (200 trees, unconstrained depth, 5-fold stratified cross-validation).
We compare against CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, DivEye \cite{diveye2026}, a 2-layer cross-entropy MLP, logistic regression, and a single-feature baseline using mean perplexity alone; results appear in \S\ref{sec:generalization}.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating generalization along seven axes: four in detail below---classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and preliminary cross-scale observations (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale})---and three in the Appendix (end-to-end learning, temperature robustness, and cross-boundary transfer).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
As a complementary ablation, we test whether depth can be estimated from surface text alone---without the targeted distributional features our pipeline extracts.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs---DeepSeek V3.2 (36.2\%, 95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (30.4\%, $[26.5, 34.6]$; each 500 samples, prompt template in Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt})---both fail to distinguish recursive depth, performing near chance (25\%).
The 42\,pp gap between RF (78.2\%) and both LLMs confirms that the depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text alone; this gap exceeds the CI half-width (${\approx}$4\,pp) by an order of magnitude, ensuring robustness at $n{=}500$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.7pp due to matched-fold evaluation.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five structurally different classifiers---spanning bagging, gradient boosting, ordinal regression, and ensemble detection---confirms the signal resides in the features, not the learning algorithm.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply: nucleus holdouts retain 30--54\%, top-$k$ holdouts fall to 8--19\%, and temperature holdouts span 3.7--39.5\% (median 8.9\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}) confirms that within-$\tau$ depth signal holds for $\tau \leq 0.9$ (d1v2 AUC $>0.80$) but collapses at $\tau{=}1.1$; cross-$\tau$ LOO transfer (mean 46.7\%) is harder than cross-strategy transfer.
Temperature variations ($\tau \in [0.5, 0.9]$) do not fundamentally alter depth discriminability within a fixed strategy; classification accuracy remains within 3pp of the $\tau{=}0.7$ baseline across all tested temperatures. Full multi-$\tau$ analysis is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}.

End-to-end PLMs (DeBERTa-v3, RoBERTa) achieve only ${\sim}$59\%, lagging RF by 19pp, confirming depth signal resides in perplexity tail statistics inaccessible to standard PLM representations (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

The preceding analyses use 1--1.5B parameter models.
To test whether depth signal persists in substantially larger generators, we extend the benchmark to two 7--8B models: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\cite{qwen2.5} and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\cite{llama3}, both under 4-bit NF4 QLoRA fine-tuning, generating depths $d \in \{0, 1, \ldots, 5\}$ with nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$, 500 samples per depth).
Feature extraction uses the same reference model (Pythia-1.4B) and 19-feature pipeline as all prior experiments.

\paragraph{Scale comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale} contrasts the two 7--8B models with the three 1--1.5B models on matched evaluation (nucleus sampling, depths 0--3).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale comparison: 4-class RF accuracy (\%, depths 0--3, nucleus), $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ (depths 0--5), and d0-vs-d1 AUC. CI: 95\% bootstrap. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:cross_scale}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & 4-class acc & $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ & d0v1 AUC \\
\midrule
GPT-2~XL (1.5B) & 83.3 & 4 & 0.999 \\
OLMo-1B & 78.8 & 4 & 0.997 \\
Pythia-1.4B & 78.2 & 4 & 0.998 \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 70.2\,[68.3, 72.4] & 4 & 0.949 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & 60.1\,[58.0, 62.1] & ${>}5$ & 0.969 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three findings emerge:

\paragraph{(1) Depth signal persists at 7--8B scale.}
Both 7--8B models substantially exceed the 25\% chance baseline: Llama-3.1-8B achieves 70.2\% (95\% CI $[68.3, 72.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B achieves 60.1\% ($[58.0, 62.1]$), confirming that recursive depth remains estimable from perplexity-based features even when the generator is 5--7$\times$ larger than the reference model.
Depth-0 separability is preserved in both models: d0v1 AUC is 0.949 (Llama-3.1) and 0.969 (Qwen2.5).

\paragraph{(2) EDD at 7--8B scale.}
At threshold $T{=}0.70$, Qwen2.5-7B maintains all consecutive-pair AUCs above 0.70 through depth~5 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale_auc}), yielding $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70} > 5$---exceeding the $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$ observed at 1--1.5B.
Llama-3.1-8B matches the smaller models at $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$: its monotonic decay causes d4v5 (0.618) to drop below $T$, despite stronger signal at shallower depths.
Both larger generators produce weaker per-step signal than the 1--1.5B models (mean consecutive AUC: 0.786 for Llama-3.1, 0.782 for Qwen2.5, vs.\ 0.852 at 1--1.5B).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Consecutive binary AUC for 7--8B models (nucleus $p{=}0.95$). Llama-3.1-8B exhibits monotonic decay (d4v5 drops below $T{=}0.70$); Qwen2.5-7B shows a non-monotonic (U-shaped) pattern with all pairs above $T$.}
\label{tab:cross_scale_auc}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & d0v1 & d1v2 & d2v3 & d3v4 & d4v5 \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 0.949 & 0.877 & 0.771 & 0.716 & 0.618 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & 0.969 & 0.729 & 0.709 & 0.732 & 0.772 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{(3) Divergent AUC trajectories.}
Llama-3.1-8B exhibits monotonic AUC decay consistent with the 1--1.5B pattern, while Qwen2.5-7B shows a U-shaped recovery beyond d2v3 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale_auc}).
This divergence suggests the U-shape is model-specific---possibly related to instruction-tuning (RLHF/DPO)---rather than scale-dependent; the Llama-3.1 result confirms monotonic decay replicates at 7--8B for at least one architecture.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
For Qwen2.5-7B, the CI lower bound (58.0\%) does not exclude sub-60\% accuracy; Llama-3.1-8B's lower bound (68.3\%) is more comfortably above chance but still below the 75--83\% range at 1--1.5B.
The divergent AUC trajectories across two models caution against strong generalizations from only two 7--8B data points: both the monotonic (Llama-3.1) and non-monotonic (Qwen2.5) patterns are single observations, and further architectures are needed to establish which is the dominant regime at this scale.
Additional confounds include 4-bit quantization, instruction-tuning, reduced sample size (500 vs.\ 5{,}000), and evaluation limited to nucleus sampling (see Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_confounds} for detailed analysis).
Cross-model recursive chains (84.75\%) and cross-domain transfer (arXiv 74.1\%) further confirm signal universality (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_boundary}).

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We formalized recursive generation depth estimation and showed that a compact feature set dominated by perplexity tail statistics achieves 70--85\% four-class accuracy ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$ across models; \S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}), with five features sufficing for 97.6\% of full performance and classifier-agnostic convergence within $\leq$0.4pp.
The dominant feature group---perplexity tail statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 under SHAP)---generalizes across the three studied model families without retraining, making depth estimation a candidate early-warning signal for model collapse~\cite{shumailov2024_nature} within this experimental scope.
Recent work corroborates this connection, identifying entropy decline as a measurable signal of recursive degradation that parallels the tail erosion our features capture~\cite{shi2025_closer_look}.
A surface-text ablation reinforces this view: zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2 at 36.2\%, Qwen2.5-7B at 30.4\%) both perform near chance despite exhibiting complementary prediction biases, and the 42\,pp gap confirms that the depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text alone (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}).

Cross-strategy transfer remains the primary bottleneck (59.1\% vs.\ 66.4\% cross-model), with the temperature-induced d1/d2 AUC inversion making single-classifier deployment across unknown strategies infeasible; cross-$\tau$ transfer is even harder (mean 46.7\%; Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}), confirming that strategy-conditioned classifiers are necessary.
A two-stage pipeline---first identifying the decoding strategy, then applying a strategy-conditioned classifier---could address this, since strategies produce distinguishable perplexity signatures; alternatively, when deployment is restricted to a single platform, the strategy is typically known a priori.

\paragraph{Depth-graduated data curation.}
Current data curation operates on a binary human-vs-machine distinction~\cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}.
Depth estimation enables a finer-grained alternative: retain low-depth generations (d1) while filtering high-depth text (d3+) where tail erosion has degraded quality.
A pilot study (Appendix~\ref{app:curation_poc}) yields an inconclusive comparison between RF-filtered and unfiltered conditions ($p>0.4$, $\mathrm{df}{=}4$), though RF-filtered subsets significantly outperform size-matched random subsampling ($p<0.001$--$0.02$); large-scale validation is needed to determine whether depth-based filtering provides a practical advantage over unfiltered training.
Cross-strategy deployment would first require strategy identification to avoid boundary inversion (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).

\paragraph{Toward a provenance toolkit.}
Depth estimation complements existing provenance methods---watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023_watermark} (requires generator cooperation) and source attribution~\cite{uchendu2020_attribution} (identifies model, not history)---by characterizing \emph{iterative reuse} regardless of generator identity~\cite{bevendorff2025_two_paradigms}.
A layered system combining watermark presence, source model, and recursive depth could support more nuanced data auditing within controlled generation settings than any single method.

\paragraph{Mixed-depth documents.}
Sliding-window depth estimation at the segment level (256--512 tokens) could extend the approach to mixed-depth documents, enabling paragraph-level curation even from partially contaminated content.

\paragraph{Adversarial robustness.}
Perplexity tail statistics exhibit inherent resistance to surface-level transformations (paraphrasing, back-translation) that preserve surprisal structure, though robustness to targeted distribution-aware attacks remains unverified.

\paragraph{Scaling behavior.}
Preliminary cross-scale observations (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}) suggest depth signal persists at 7--8B scale (Llama-3.1-8B: 70.2\%, Qwen2.5-7B: 60.1\%) with weaker per-step signal (mean AUC ${\approx}$0.78 vs.\ ${\approx}$0.85 at 1--1.5B), though four confounds preclude strong conclusions.
The divergent AUC trajectories (monotonic vs.\ U-shaped) suggest instruction tuning may alter degradation dynamics; further scaling may require richer feature sets to maintain discriminability.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 69.6\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.4\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (76.6--85.4\%), followed by OLMo (74.2--82.0\%) and Pythia (69.6--78.2\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 8.4~pp, comparable to the 7.1~pp mean within-strategy model gap---decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

